{ The flagship Gligoric IQP mainline: Black liquidates the center into a classic d4 isolated pawn structure, and instead of automatic 10. Bg5 we steer into modern 10. Qe2! — keeping our pieces flexible while scoring 71% online. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 d5 6. Nf3 c5 { 6... c5 challenges our center directly. Notice three rarer move-6 tries before Black commits ...c5 — passive 6... c6, 6... Nbd7, and the tactical trap 6... Ne4: } { [%cal Gc4d5] } ( 6... c6 7. O-O Nbd7 8. Qb3 { — Passive 6... c6 leaves Black cramped; 8. Qb3 hits b4 and eyes d5 with a clean +0.28 edge.<br><br>What if 6... Nbd7?: } ) ( 6... Nbd7 7. O-O dxc4 8. Bxc4 c5 { — 7. O-O keeps normal IQP or hanging-pawn tension without giving away tempo.<br><br>And watch the tactical refutation of 6... Ne4?!: } ) ( 6... Ne4 $6 7. O-O Nxc3 8. bxc3 dxc4 9. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 10. cxb4 { — 9. Bxh7+! is the classic tactical shot — after 9... Kxh7 10. cxb4 White emerges a clean pawn up at +1.76! } ) 7. O-O { 7. O-O gets our king to safety before declaring the center. } { [%cal Bf1e1,Bc1g5] } 7... cxd4 { 7... cxd4 begins the IQP liquidation. Note that Black can also sidestep ...cxd4 with 7... b6 or 7... Bxc3: } ( 7... b6 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Ne5 { — 7... b6 transposes into Line 1.5's 7... c5 sideline at 0.00 to +0.11.<br><br>If Black gives up the dark-squared bishop with 7... Bxc3?: } ) ( 7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Qc7 { — 8. bxc3 repairs the structure and hands White an uncontested bishop pair at +0.48. } ) 8. exd4 { Exchange check: on 7... cxd4, recapturing 8. exd4 is virtually forced — opening the semi-open e-file and c1-h6 diagonal while freeing our dark-squared bishop. Taking with the knight on d4 drops central control. } { [%csl Gd4] [%cal Bc1g5,Bf1e1] } 8... dxc4 { 8... dxc4 yields the center into an IQP structure. If Black skips 8... dxc4 and plays 8... Nc6 or 8... Bxc3, White grabs immediate space: } ( 8... Nc6 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Bc2 { — 10. Bc2!, +0.54, avoids trading into equality and eyes the b1-h7 diagonal.<br><br>And 8... Bxc3?: } ) ( 8... Bxc3 9. bxc3 Qc7 10. Ne5 { — 10. Ne5!, +0.76, plants a monster knight on e5 with strong kingside attacking prospects. } ) 9. Bxc4 { 9. Bxc4 wins back the pawn cleanly and opens the c-file, turning d4 into a dynamic isolated queen's pawn. } { [%csl Yd4] [%cal Bc4b3,Bc1g5] } 9... b6 { 9... b6 prepares ...Bb7 to fight over d5 and e4. Check both 9... Nc6 and 9... Nbd7 below: } ( 9... Nc6 10. Bg5 Be7 11. Re1 { — 9... Nc6 transposes directly into Line 1.4.<br><br>If Black develops 9... Nbd7 first?: } ) ( 9... Nbd7 10. Qe2 b6 11. d5 { — 10. Qe2 unifies against ...Nbd7 and ...b6 — and if Black still plays 10... b6, 11. d5! strikes the center immediately for +0.54! } ) 10. Qe2 $1 { 10. Qe2! is our sharp modern choice over autopilot 10. Bg5. Why skip 10. Bg5? It scores only 39% after 10... Bb7 — 46% overall — because Black meets it calmly with ...Bb7 and ...Bxc3, whereas 10. Qe2 clears d1 for Rd1, reinforces c4 along the f1-a6 diagonal while Bc4 eyes e6 and f7, and keeps d4-d5 ready. } { [%csl Ye2,Re6] [%cal Be2c4,Bf1d1] } ( 10. Bg5 Bb7 11. Re1 Nbd7 { — Classical Kramnik vs Kasparov path — objectively 0.00, though 10. Qe2! keeps more practical bite online. } ) 10... Bb7 11. Rd1 { 11. Rd1 centralizes our rook behind the d4 pawn, eyeing the d8 queen through the center and preparing d4-d5 breakthroughs.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: 0.00 to +0.17 — Honest Balanced Dynamic d4 IQP Battle<br><br>• Our Plan — Prepare d4-d5 when Black plays ...h6 or ...Nbd7, plant Ne5 or Bg5, and lift a3 against ...Bb4 pins.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black wants ...Bxc3 bxc3 followed by ...Nbd7 or ...Nc6-a5 to blockade c4 and d5; watch the d4 pawn carefully against ...Bxf3.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Avoid minor-piece trades while keeping the d4 IQP unless d4-d5 liquidates into a superior bishop or rook endgame.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Explore White's dynamic d4-d5 and Bg5 continuations after 11. Rd1: } { [%csl Gd4,Yd5,Rc4] [%cal Gd4d5,Bf3e5,Bc1g5,Rb4c3] } ( 11. Rd1 Nbd7 12. d5 exd5 13. Nxd5 { — Immediate 12. d5! blows open the center before Black coordinates — +0.17 with active piece pressure.<br><br>And if Black inserts 11... h6 prophylaxis?: } ) ( 11. Rd1 h6 12. Be3 Nbd7 13. d5 { — White calmly develops Be3 and still breaks with 13. d5! in an honest 0.00 struggle. } ) *

