{ When Black takes on c4 on move 7 and develops 8... Nc6, immediate 9. a3! forces the b4 bishop to declare itself right away — scoring 56% online across 48 games. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 d5 6. Nf3 c5 7. O-O dxc4 8. Bxc4 Nc6 { 8... Nc6 pressures d4 without releasing the c5-d4 tension yet. Notice Black's three other move-8 tries — 8... cxd4 transposes directly into Line 1.1, while 8... a6 and 8... b6 aim for queenside counterplay: } { [%cal Ga2a3] } ( 8... cxd4 9. exd4 b6 10. Qe2 { — 53% online reply: transposes straight back into Line 1.1.<br><br>What if Black prepares ...b5 with 8... a6?: } ) ( 8... a6 9. a4 cxd4 10. exd4 Nc6 { — 9. a4 clamps ...b5 immediately before settling into an active IQP.<br><br>And against 8... b6?: } ) ( 8... b6 9. Qe2 cxd4 10. exd4 Bb7 { — 10. Qe2! steers right back into Line 1.1. } ) 9. a3 $1 { 9. a3! asks the b4 bishop an immediate question before Black stabilizes with ...a6 or ...cxd4. } { [%csl Ya3,Rb4] [%cal Ga3b4] } 9... Ba5 { Retreating 9... Ba5 keeps the bishop pair at the cost of leaving it far from the kingside, while 9... Bxc3 or 9... cxd4 hand White clear structural assets: } ( 9... Bxc3 10. bxc3 Qc7 11. Bd3 { — Exchange check: 10. bxc3 repairs our center and hands White the bishop pair at +0.06 to +0.21.<br><br>And if Black inserts 9... cxd4?: } ) ( 9... cxd4 10. axb4 dxc3 11. bxc3 { — Opening the a-file and claiming the bishop pair after 10. axb4 gives White a crisp +0.48 to +0.52 edge. } ) 10. Qd3 { 10. Qd3 defends c4, clears d1 for Rd1, and steps off the d-file while eyeing h7 once the center opens. } { [%cal Bd3c4,Bd3h7] } 10... cxd4 11. exd4 { Exchange check: on 10... cxd4, recapturing 11. exd4 opens the e-file and c1-h6 diagonal while creating a dynamic d4 IQP. Never take with 11. Nxd4? — it falls to the 11... Ne5! fork of Qd3 and Bc4 at -1.06.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.15 to +0.26 — Active IQP with Queen-Bishop Battery<br><br>• Our Plan — Play Bf4 or Bg5, Rd1 or Rac1, and meet ...Bb6 with Be3 or Ba2; look for d4-d5 when Black's a5 bishop is offside.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black plays ...h6 to stop Bg5, ...Bb6 to pressure d4, or ...Ne7-d5 to blockade d4; keep Ba2 and Rad1 handy.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Preserve our light-squared c4 bishop, tucking it to a2 if needed, and only trade our dark-squared e3 bishop on b6 when ...axb6 opens the a-file favorably.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>White's harmonious IQP buildup against 11... h6 and 11... Bb6: } { [%csl Gd4,Ra5] [%cal Bc1f4,Bc4a2,Bf1d1,Ba5b6] } ( 11. exd4 h6 12. Bf4 Bd7 13. Rac1 { — Stockfish #1: 12. Bf4 claims the h2-b8 diagonal and prepares Rad1 and d5 — +0.26.<br><br>If Black drops the bishop back to b6 right away?: } ) ( 11. exd4 Bb6 12. Be3 h6 13. Ba2 { — 12. Be3 overprotects d4 cleanly and keeps the center solid at +0.39. } ) *

