{ The Bernstein Defense 7... Nc6 is Black's #1 online choice at 39%. Instead of autopilot 8. a3, surgical 8. cxd5! exd5 9. dxc5! isolates Black's d5 pawn and nets +0.28 to +0.35. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 d5 6. Nf3 c5 7. O-O Nc6 8. cxd5 $1 { 8. cxd5! is Stockfish's #1 choice, +0.34 to +0.39, beating routine 8. a3 at +0.13 by forcing an asymmetrical isolated d5 pawn. Why not 8. a3? After 8. a3 Bxc3 9. bxc3, Black hits back comfortably with ...Qc7 or ...c4. } { [%csl Yd5] [%cal Gd4c5,Bc1g5] } 8... exd5 { 8... exd5 accepts the isolated d5 pawn. If Black avoids 8... exd5 with 8... Nxd5 or 8... cxd4, White punishes both cleanly: } ( 8... Nxd5 9. Nxd5 Qxd5 10. Qc2 { — Exchange check: trading on d5 and landing 10. Qc2! hits c5 and h7 simultaneously, surging to +1.96!<br><br>What if 8... cxd4?: } ) ( 8... cxd4 9. exd4 Nxd5 10. Bc2 { — 9. exd4 gives an open IQP battle where 10. Bc2!, +0.54, builds a Queen-Bishop battery against h7. } ) 9. dxc5 { 9. dxc5 gives White the semi-open d-file while 9... Bxc5 opens the c-file, leaving Black's d5 pawn permanently isolated. } { [%csl Rd5] [%cal Gf3d4,Bc1g5] } 9... Bxc5 { 9... Bxc5 recaptures actively. Notice Black's alternative 9... Bg4 before recapturing on c5: } ( 9... Bg4 10. Be2 Bxc5 11. b3 { — Calm 10. Be2 unpins f3 cleanly and prepares b3-Bb2 targeting d5 — +0.11 to +0.19. } ) 10. h3 { 10. h3 is essential prophylaxis — denying ...Bg4 and keeping f3 comfortable before we launch b3 and Bb2. } { [%csl Yg4] [%cal Gb2b3,Bc1b2] } 10... Re8 11. b3 { 11. b3 prepares Bb2 to laser straight at d5 and f6, while keeping Na4 ideas in reserve if Black plays ...Bd6 or ...Bb4.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.24 to +0.36 — Textbook Siege Against Black's Isolated d5 Pawn<br><br>• Our Plan — Fianchetto Bb2, plant Nd4 or Nce2-d4 to blockade d4, or route Nce2-f4 to pressure d5, and play Rc1/Nb5 against Black's exposed dark squares.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black jumps ...Ne4 to trade minor pieces, pushes ...d4 to liquidate the IQP, or swings ...Qd6-h6; meet ...d4 with Na4 or exd4 safely.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Every minor-piece trade weakens Black's protection of d5; entering a rook or minor-piece endgame against d5 favors White strongly.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>White's clean blockading responses to Black's active move-11 tries: } { [%csl Rd5,Bb2] [%cal Bc1b2,Ra1c1,Bf3d4,Rc6e4] } ( 11. b3 Ne4 12. Bb2 Bf5 13. Rc1 { — 12. Bb2 controls d4 and e5 cleanly while Rc1 eyeballs the open c-file — +0.24.<br><br>And if Black tries the sharp central liquidation 11... d4?: } ) ( 11. b3 d4 12. Na4 Bf8 13. exd4 { — 12. Na4 kicks the c5 bishop with tempo before capturing on d4 — White keeps a healthy extra pawn at +0.35. } ) *

